<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  iOS bundle Info.plist extras. Tauri merges this file (picked up by convention
  from src-tauri/) into the generated iOS Info.plist. NSMicrophoneUsageDescription
  is required for mic access — accessing the mic without it crashes the app.

  UIApplicationSceneManifest / UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes enables native
  multi-window via UIScene (MJX-142): a chat session opens as its own scene —
  side-by-side on iPad, replacing on iPhone. tao/wry supply the UISceneDelegate
  programmatically, so only the "supports multiple scenes" flag is needed here; no
  UISceneConfigurations entry. `app.supports_multiple_windows()` and tao's
  scene-request path both read this key.

  NSAppTransportSecurity lets the WEBVIEW load a cleartext gateway. Without it ATS
  refuses every http:// load in WKWebView silently — `webview.navigate()` returns Ok
  (the call only queues a message), the page never changes, and the interactive
  sign-in polls for a cookie that can never arrive until its 120s budget runs out.
  It looked like "tapping Sign in does nothing", and it is the one failure the Rust
  side cannot see: reqwest uses raw sockets, not NSURLSession, so ATS never touches
  it and /api/status, /api/auth/me and the whole gateway connection work over http
  regardless. Only the login page was blocked.

  Android has had the same opt-in all along — gen/android/app/build.gradle.kts sets
  `usesCleartextTraffic` true for the debug buildType — which is precisely why this
  flow worked there and not here.

  NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent is the narrowest key that can work: it lifts ATS
  for WKWebView ONLY, leaving NSURLSession under full ATS. NSExceptionDomains is not
  an option — the gateway host is whatever the user typed (any LAN address, any
  Tailscale name), so there is no static list to write. NSAllowsLocalNetworking does
  not cover it either: it reaches .local, unqualified names and loopback, but not raw
  private-IP literals, and Tailscale's 100.64.0.0/10 is CGNAT rather than local
  networking at all. It is kept alongside for the .local / loopback cases.

  App Store note: NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent can draw a justification request
  at review. The justification is real — this app connects to gateways its user runs
  on their own private network, which cannot carry a publicly trusted certificate.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Hermes uses the microphone for voice conversations.</string>
  <key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
  <dict>
    <key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent</key>
    <true/>
    <key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
  <key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
  <dict>
    <key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
